# Notify when the dotfiles repo has commits this machine hasn't applied.
# Deliberately read-only: it fetches and compares, it never pulls or applies.
# An unattended `chezmoi update` can restart services and re-run scripts at an
# arbitrary moment — including the secret decryption, which would pop a YubiKey
# PIN prompt with no terminal to answer it.
#
# Enabled automatically by
# .chezmoiscripts/run_after_11-enable-update-check.sh.tmpl
[Unit]
Description=Check whether the dotfiles repo has unapplied commits
# No network ordering, and the two lines that used to claim it were removed
# rather than corrected: there is no user-instance network-online.target, so
# `After=/Wants=` on it never ordered anything here. A fetch that runs before
# the network is up fails and the check stays silent, which is the same
# outcome the ordering was meant to buy. See the sibling comment in
# dotfiles-secrets-check.service.

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/dotfiles-update-check
# No SuccessExitStatus here either, and for a duller reason than its sibling:
# `SuccessExitStatus=0` is systemd's default, so the line asserted nothing. What
# it was trying to say is true and stays true — no network, no remote and a
# shallow clone are ordinary states, and the script exits 0 on all of them
# itself, which is where that behaviour belongs.
